#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""b3r6 audit entry (experiment b3r6-cut-trail-audit of the package's experiments/manifest.json).

The organizer harness runs this file inside its pinned Docker sandbox, twice with the
same request, and recomputes every returned message pair with its own copy of the
selected target digest.  What it does per run:

  1. executes the *embedded, verbatim* copies of the generator (bsgen.py), the ledger
     (ledger.py) and the distinguished-point table (table.py) that produced the package,
     so the emitted instruction list, the recovery circuit and every ledger row are
     recomputed from source and compared with the constants below (gate count,
     operations per batch, program sha256, total N, score);
  2. evaluates the first round of the search (256 seeded points) with the emitted
     straight-line program and compares **every one of the 256 bits** of every lane
     with the scalar reference of the same generator (not a masked comparison);
  3. accounts the memory *as implemented*: it packs every gate into the claimed 4-byte
     record, fills the shipped open-addressing table with 35000 real 48-bit keys and
     looks every one of them up by key (bounded linear probing), recomputes the peak
     live plane count and the setup-phase peak, and checks both against the ledger;
  4. uses each organizer trial seed as the algorithmic coin that generates that trial's
     candidate start points, evaluates all points of a round with the emitted bit-sliced
     program, and detects equal keys **through the shipped table** (the same insert /
     duplicate / bounded-probe path the attack uses), returning for each trial two
     distinct 64-byte messages K || x, K = 0^32, whose digests agree on the low
     8 bits (finding EXP-F2, LC-TABLE-1).
The program never writes to disk, never reads a path, and makes no network access.
"""
